00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:21.890
So the business issue.

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:24.100
So VAT and GST--

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:26.350
so Value Added Tax,
predominant tax

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:30.880
on consumption in Europe
and in most of Latin America

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.500
and in most of Asia-Pacific.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:34.660
It's called Goods and
Services Tax, GST,

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:37.120
in some of those jurisdictions.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:39.220
It's a little like
sales tax in the US,

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:41.080
except that it's two-sided.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:46.630
So not only do businesses that
sell goods and services have

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:49.720
to collect and pay tax to
the authorities on the things

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:50.840
they sell--

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:54.250
so that is a feature of
the GST and VAT regime.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:57.610
The critical difference between
GST and VAT and sales tax,

00:05:57.610 --> 00:05:59.890
however, is that-- and this
is the two-sided nature

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:01.060
of the tax--

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:04.870
is that you as a
business are also

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:09.460
charged VAT and GST on
the things that you buy.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:15.000
So it's an input and
an output system.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.070
The good news for businesses
is that, for the vast majority

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:23.420
of the purchases that businesses
make, the VAT or the GST

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:24.950
can be reclaimed.

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:28.790
It can be recovered as a
refund from the tax offices.

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:31.530
But, this is subject to a
couple of key conditions,

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.800
and this is where the
manual problems have existed

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:38.390
in our world since the 1970s
when the tax was conceived

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:40.440
by the French.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.310
The two key conditions
to be able to get

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:46.400
that GST or VAT that's been
incurred on your purchases

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:49.580
back from the tax
authorities, one,

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:53.810
that it is a specified
business-eligible purchase

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:56.720
under local VAT and GST law.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:59.880
So there is a legal requirement
that you need to meet.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:02.870
And that's based on tax
rules and legislation.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.960
That business and
tax legislation--

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.590
those tax rules-- are then
augmented or qualified

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:11.570
by the type of
business that you are.

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:15.440
So there's a legal question,
is it or isn't it recoverable?

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:18.560
And then, the second question
is, based on the business type

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:22.400
that I am as an acquirer
of services or goods,

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:26.690
how does that impact or change
my recoverability of that tax?

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:28.100
So that's the technical problem.

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:29.840
And in 150 countries
around the world,

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.150
those rules are all different.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:35.720
The second problem is
a document problem,

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:38.910
and we we're lucky enough to
kind of hear some of the Q&amp;A

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:41.910
and the interviews over in
Moscone just before we went

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:42.410
live.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:45.580
And one of the ladies was
talking about dark data,

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:48.740
and that's data that is
trapped on a paper document

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:50.330
in an unstructured form.

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:53.570
And that's the problem that
we have in the VAT world

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:54.950
on purchases.

00:07:54.950 --> 00:08:00.800
We need to be able to identify
that we hold a valid document

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:02.540
to support our claim.

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:04.787
So one, we need to make
sure we're legal in terms

00:08:04.787 --> 00:08:05.870
of complying with the law.

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:08.900
Two, we need to check that
against our business type.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:11.640
And three, regardless of
what goes into our system,

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:16.250
we need to hold a valid
document in a specified form.

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:20.720
And so, what that has meant
in terms of business process

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:25.730
is that, typically, VAT
and GST on purchases

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:28.610
has been systemized to a degree.

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:33.110
And that is effectively once the
data is input into the system.

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:37.190
So when a purchase is
made, a PO is struck,

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:39.080
and an invoice is received--

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:41.635
either a vendor invoice
or an employee expense,

00:08:41.635 --> 00:08:43.760
there's a big piece here
around employee expenses--

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:50.450
somebody has to actually lift
the data off those invoices,

00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:53.540
make a GST or VAT decision--
what kind of expense

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:56.600
it is, is it recoverable,
and if it is recoverable

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:59.510
what code do I then
apply to the transaction

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:03.470
as I'm posting that
transaction into the system.

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:05.600
Once the transaction
is in the system,

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:08.210
systems are very, very
good at then automating

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:10.490
how the GST should then
flow through the accounts

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:12.290
and then ultimately
onto a return

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:16.080
so that you can claim it
back from the regulators.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:18.170
So that's what effectively
we mean by a tax engine

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:18.890
up on the screen.

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:21.920
So tax engines are
really, really good

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:25.310
at running rules-based logic
to identify and capture

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:28.910
GST or VAT, but
the key criteria is

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:32.040
that we capture that data
off of those source invoices.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:35.450
And that old adage
of junk in and junk

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:38.180
out is really, really
true from a VAT and GST

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:41.075
perspective on purchases.

00:09:41.075 --> 00:09:42.700
So what we find, as
we've talked about,

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:46.020
is a very manual process
across a number of our clients

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:47.320
across the world.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.680
And that could be a native
accounts payable function

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:50.850
that sits on-site.

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:54.280
It can be a BPO, an
outsourced function,

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:58.230
but there is always inevitably
some form of document checking,

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.870
manual input, and process.

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:06.360
And what we see is it creates a
lot of issues for our clients.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:10.110
The first issue
is it costs money.

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:14.220
And I've got a stat up
there from firm SAP Concur

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.320
they estimate across
the VAT world,

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:22.320
GST world there's around $20
billion US of VAT and GST

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:26.700
on taxes, on hotels,
on flights, on meals

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:27.953
that just goes missing.

00:10:27.953 --> 00:10:29.370
And the reason why
it goes missing

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:37.040
is because, one, businesses see
these as low-cost purchases.

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:38.760
But they do add up
over time, obviously,

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.460
given the number on the screen.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:46.230
Two, the employees often that
are making reimbursement claims

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:49.680
need to make those tax
decisions off of the invoices.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:53.490
And three, because of that, the
risk of an employee selecting

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.770
a wrong tax code
is simply too high,

00:10:55.770 --> 00:11:00.757
so businesses choose not to
engage and claim that GST back.

00:11:00.757 --> 00:11:02.340
The second thing in
the vendor world--

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:04.350
so outside of the
employee expense world,

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:06.180
in the vendor purchases world--

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.460
we see a lot of problems,
particularly in Australia,

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:12.200
around that manual process
risk on accounts payable.

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:14.910
And some of the stats out
of our Australian tax office

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:18.900
actually show that almost
70% of errors on the accounts

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:19.808
payable side--

00:11:19.808 --> 00:11:21.600
and this is data
collected across the whole

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:23.160
of the Australian
taxpayer base--

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:27.040
are caused by system
and process deficiency.

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:28.133
So there's risk.

00:11:28.133 --> 00:11:29.050
That can go both ways.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:32.310
You can overclaim or underclaim
because of the manual gap.

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.770
And then, three, efficiency.

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:40.500
So you're asking in this manual
world accounts payable and BPOs

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:44.370
who aren't often tax trained to
make tax decisions and process

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:46.720
documents for tax purposes.

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.390
And that takes time
and effort that

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:50.940
can be redeployed to other
more value-adding tasks

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:53.440
in a business.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:56.260
The last thing
that I just wanted

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:58.660
to touch on in terms
of the current state

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:01.570
and why we saw this is a
really key problem to solve

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:03.910
is that the VAT
and the GST world

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:07.360
is undergoing a huge
shift at the moment, which

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:09.610
is driven by technology.

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:13.750
So as it stands, in the majority
of the VAT and GST world

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:16.570
at the moment, it's a
traditional tax compliance

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:17.660
reporting system.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:20.590
And what I mean by that is
transactions occur in a period,

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:21.790
in a month.

00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:23.140
The month stops.

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:25.750
We've got 21 days
to then collate

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:27.580
all of that data
out of our system,

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:30.530
put it onto a tax
return, and file.

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:32.980
And in that 21-day
period between month end

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:34.600
and when we need
to file, there's

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:37.930
time for us to run checks,
to extract reports,

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:41.350
to do reconciliations,
and to file returns.

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:43.360
So we've got time to
look back over our data

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:46.320
and check that it's right.

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:48.400
The problem is, where
we're now moving,

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:51.730
is to what I call a real-time
reporting electronic filing

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:52.840
regime.

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:55.690
And this is no more pronounced
than in places like Spain,

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:58.210
where the regulator now
says we're doing away

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:02.320
with that VAT return traditional
process and we're replacing it.

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:04.300
And what we're
replacing it with is,

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:08.230
every four days, I want a
direct upload out of your source

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:12.400
systems as a client, and I
want your AR, I want your IP,

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:16.190
I want you GL, and I'll work out
how much tax you have to pay.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:17.980
And I'll do it every
four days, and you

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:20.300
have to pay it every four days.

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:22.210
So you can see that
fundamental shift

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:26.110
from a time lag between
when a transaction occurs

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:28.800
and when we need to report and
we need to collect and pay,

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:30.970
to now that being
much more compressed

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:33.330
and the tax offices
across the world having

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:36.850
a lot more of your data
than previously you gave.

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:39.190
So for us, what that
means is it really,

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:42.820
really enhances this importance
of getting those tax decisions

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:45.630
right in the system first time.

00:13:45.630 --> 00:13:47.590
And so, we think Zipline
does a lot of that.

00:13:47.590 --> 00:13:52.990
We think it fills that last
mile between invoice receipt

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:55.120
from third-party
suppliers, from employees

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:58.810
who want their expenses
back, into the system

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:01.360
to allow tax rules and
classification to work

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:03.010
to get your data
right in the system

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:05.290
at the time the
transactions are processed.

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:06.790
And I might just
hand over to Ed now

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:08.207
just to give you
a little bit more

00:14:08.207 --> 00:14:11.710
overview in terms
of the solution

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:14.500
and where we're at both
as a standalone offering,

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:16.780
but also some of the
exciting opportunities

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:18.760
that we're looking at
the moment in terms

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:21.940
of partnerships and
integrations with ERP providers

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:24.140
and expense platforms like SAP.

00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:26.800
So for example, we're in
the middle of conversations

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:30.730
right now with SAP Concur to
have integration capability

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:34.210
to actually have this
as an ongoing subprocess

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:36.270
within your employee
expenses system--

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:40.180
similar also for the accounts
payable modules in the core

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:41.640
ERP providers.

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:43.520
So at the moment, a
standalone solution,

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:46.062
but we're really, really excited
about what integration looks

00:14:46.062 --> 00:14:49.420
like and having this ingrained
as a subprocess for tax

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:51.610
inside of the ERP in the future.

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:01.640
EDWARD KNUST:
Morning, everybody.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:03.890
We've got the solution
overview on the left-hand side.

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:06.680
As Brady mentioned, I'm
not a tech guy either,

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:08.210
but I can talk it
through basically

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:11.930
what the technology stack is.

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:14.460
I think the key points are
on the right-hand side.

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:15.600
So what are we doing?

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:18.500
We're extracting the data
from the underlying source

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:25.010
invoices utilizing
Google's Cloud Vision API,

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:29.570
and we're also using a
machine learning base model

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:33.620
to take that OCR output
and to classify the fields

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:36.793
on the invoice or the receipt.

00:15:36.793 --> 00:15:38.210
As Brady mentioned
before, we then

00:15:38.210 --> 00:15:40.760
have to validate whether
what's on that invoice

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:42.450
meets local requirements.

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:44.210
So there's a set
of tax rules which

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:48.380
we've built in from each local
jurisdiction as to what fields

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:51.377
are and are not required.

00:15:51.377 --> 00:15:53.210
Part three is where it
starts getting really

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:56.690
interesting because, having
then taken those data fields,

00:15:56.690 --> 00:16:00.650
having applied the ML logic
to get the relevant data

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:05.720
fields categorized, we can
then run PWC classification

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:07.610
over what that expense type is.

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:10.520
So as Brady mentioned before,
different expense types

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:13.680
are capable of
different tax credits.

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:16.220
So the capability
we've built in here

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:18.950
allows for an expense
type determination

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:21.650
and differentiation
between that expense that's

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:26.350
been incurred where it's
relevant for tax recovery.

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:29.740
And lastly, we get a
validated tax reclaim amount

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:31.785
per transaction line item.

00:16:31.785 --> 00:16:33.910
So if there hasn't been a
booking in the underlying

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:37.750
system, that will be the
GST reclaim amount or VAT.

00:16:37.750 --> 00:16:41.440
If there has been, then we can
reconcile the Zipline output

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:43.272
with the underlying
system output.

00:16:43.272 --> 00:16:44.980
To the extent that
there is a difference,

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:48.180
that can be investigated.

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:50.840
So if we look at
the solution itself,

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:52.340
I think the key
point is that we've

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:54.150
got those two different models.

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:56.270
So for invoice
extraction, we've got

00:16:56.270 --> 00:17:00.920
an ML model that sits over
the Cloud Vision API data

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:01.880
extraction.

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:04.970
We also use the Cloud
Translate capability where

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:07.400
the invoice is not in English.

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:10.460
And we have that separate
model for the classification

00:17:10.460 --> 00:17:13.530
in the bottom right-hand
corner of the expense type.

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:16.910
So for those who are interested,
the model that we're using

00:17:16.910 --> 00:17:23.119
is a long short-term memory
recurrent neural network.

00:17:23.119 --> 00:17:24.599
I think I got my memory right.

00:17:27.198 --> 00:17:27.990
BRADY DEVER: Sorry.

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:30.450
Just to jump in before
we jump into the, demo

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:32.880
I think that's a really
important point in terms

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:37.160
of those four gates, in
terms of the business case

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:39.660
that we started to think about
nine months ago when we first

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:41.790
saw some of this
technology in use.

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:44.940
So there are a lot of OCR--

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:48.960
as many of you in the room will
know-- a lot of OCR providers

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:51.870
that look at
expense posting data

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:55.080
extraction from unstructured
into structured.

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:56.880
The thing that we
really like about this--

00:17:56.880 --> 00:17:58.560
and particularly the
Google technology

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:00.340
that sits behind it
in the Vision API

00:18:00.340 --> 00:18:02.130
and the deep learning models--

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:04.540
is that, unlike a number
of other OCR providers,

00:18:04.540 --> 00:18:07.890
which takes a template or
grid-based approach to OCR

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:09.900
where we teach the
machine to look for things

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:12.180
in certain quadrants
of a document,

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:14.022
the benefit of the
Google technology

00:18:14.022 --> 00:18:16.230
here is that, because of
the deep learning model that

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:19.200
sits behind it and the hundreds
of thousands of documents that

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:21.540
have set in the
training data set,

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:23.520
is that the tool keeps
looking no matter

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:26.850
where the data is
located on a document

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:29.050
to get the thing that we need.

00:18:29.050 --> 00:18:30.810
So that's the first
thing that I really

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:34.380
like about the Google tech
and the differentiator

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:35.093
that it gives.

00:18:35.093 --> 00:18:36.510
The second thing,
which is where I

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:39.300
think the incremental value
comes off some of the existing

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:42.840
OCR processes for tax, is that
not only does it just extract

00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:44.790
for the purposes of posting.

00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:45.540
It extracts.

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:47.730
And while it's extracting,
it apply smart logic

00:18:47.730 --> 00:18:49.830
to work out whether
the document is

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:52.890
valid from a tax perspective--
to solve that point we

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:54.540
spoke about before.

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:58.710
And then, it also does
things like pings internet

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:01.890
registers for tax authorities
to validate that the seller is

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:05.105
who they say they are, that
their ABN or their VAT ID

00:19:05.105 --> 00:19:05.975
is correct--

00:19:05.975 --> 00:19:07.350
so they are
genuinely registered.

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:10.920
And that matters in a
lot of jurisdictions.

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:13.620
And it also validates the
GST calculations or the VAT

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:14.380
calculation.

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:19.830
So are we seeing a $10 GST
amount for $100 purchase.

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:22.270
In Australia, the
VAT rate is 10%.

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:26.280
So it is doing a whole bunch of
smart calcs using the ML, based

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:29.010
on tax rules and logic that
PWC has co-created together

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:31.650
with Google, to
actually lift the data

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:34.410
and do smart things to
it on the invoice level.

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:36.072
So that's gate one.

00:19:36.072 --> 00:19:38.280
Then, once we get through
the invoice validity phase,

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:40.560
we then kick it into
another ML model,

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:44.580
which is a tax engine which
actually runs categorisation--

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:47.085
so looking at, for example,
if we've acquired stools--

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:53.040
how does the body of data
that sits in the ML model

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:55.830
categorize stools
for VAT purposes?

00:19:55.830 --> 00:19:56.920
Office equipment.

00:19:56.920 --> 00:19:59.910
So it was a stools
equals office equipment.

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:02.310
And that will then do
the order categorisation,

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:05.010
which then allows
the standardization

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:07.800
to occur for a tax
calculation rules set

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:10.180
to then do the rest of
the job in the system.

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:13.440
And so, the smart
technology that Google

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:15.480
has in Vision API and
their deep learning

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:18.570
models, the TensorFlow models
that sit behind the tax rules

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:22.770
engine augmented and
supplemented PWC tax content

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:24.752
for us is where the
magic happens in this.

00:20:24.752 --> 00:20:26.460
And we're really,
really keen to show you

00:20:26.460 --> 00:20:30.010
how this works with a
few real-life scenarios.

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:35.010
But, that I think is the key
differentiator between this POC

00:20:35.010 --> 00:20:38.550
and what other OCR providers on
the expense side in the market

00:20:38.550 --> 00:20:39.630
do today.

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:41.910
We actually add some
smart analysis at the time

00:20:41.910 --> 00:20:44.880
that the OCR is
scanning the invoices

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:46.950
and processing those invoices.

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:47.490
Sorry.

00:20:47.490 --> 00:20:48.740
EDWARD KNUST: No, that's fine.

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:52.738
So if we can move on
to the next process,

00:20:52.738 --> 00:20:54.780
which outlines what the
business process actually

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:56.040
is at the moment.

00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:58.620
If we have a look at
the top line here,

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:02.080
this is what's happening within
the expense management system.

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:04.600
So we've got data
going into the system.

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:07.500
We've got typically an AP
clerk or an employee making

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:09.550
those classification decisions.

00:21:09.550 --> 00:21:12.100
Where Zipline comes in
is that second line.

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:15.030
So as Brady mentioned,
the TensorFlow models,

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:18.570
the Cloud Vision API, the
Translate API where required

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:19.980
will kick in.

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:23.340
And then, that triggers
the automated process

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:26.820
of that validation
classification

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:29.610
and then tax credit
calculation, which can then

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:32.910
feed automatically
onto a tax return

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:34.860
or in a live
reporting environment

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:36.643
direct to the tax authority.

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:43.070
So with that, I'll
jump into the demo

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:44.840
and show you exactly
how it works.

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:51.950
Excellent.

00:21:51.950 --> 00:21:55.760
So you will notice that
the UI has not been

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:57.440
designed as a client-facing UI.

00:21:57.440 --> 00:22:00.740
At this point in time,
this is an internal PWC UI.

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:02.780
We are developing client UI.

00:22:02.780 --> 00:22:05.660
But for the purpose of
the proof of concept,

00:22:05.660 --> 00:22:07.820
we kept this as an internal one.

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:09.202
But certainly,
our anchor clients

00:22:09.202 --> 00:22:10.910
are very interested
in the visualizations

00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:12.368
that we can take
from this process.

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:16.910
So the first step is
going to be to get

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:19.620
the documents into the system.

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:24.420
So here, I'm going to
select five invoices.

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:27.950
We're going to upload those
invoices into the system.

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:30.290
You can see that that
happened very quickly.

00:22:30.290 --> 00:22:34.020
We are unconstrained by volume.

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:36.260
So to give you an example,
one of our anchor clients

00:22:36.260 --> 00:22:39.560
has several hundred
thousand invoices.

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:42.860
Again, the power of the
Google tech allows for the OCR

00:22:42.860 --> 00:22:46.140
to happen in a matter
of milliseconds.

00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:49.610
So we're completely
unconstrained by data upload.

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:54.710
We are working with partners
such as SAP and SAP Concur

00:22:54.710 --> 00:22:58.190
on utilizing their APIs to
go direct into their systems

00:22:58.190 --> 00:23:02.630
because of bulk data extraction
that using their APIs

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:04.100
is most efficient.

00:23:04.100 --> 00:23:07.910
That said, we also have
developed RPA solutions

00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:10.640
to allow for bulk
extraction of data

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:12.486
where an API is not available.

00:23:15.820 --> 00:23:17.820
So those files have
now been uploaded.

00:23:17.820 --> 00:23:20.190
You can see that we
have each of them here.

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:22.380
To the extent a file
has been multiple pages,

00:23:22.380 --> 00:23:26.760
it's automatically split
it into individual pages.

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:30.270
We can now go in and look
at a particular invoice.

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:33.840
So here, we've got an
Australian invoice.

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:35.820
It is a valid tax invoice.

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:39.390
So we'll show you how the
OCR process has worked.

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:43.805
So the OCR has identified each
of the individual text blocks

00:23:43.805 --> 00:23:44.430
on the invoice.

00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:49.730
Once it goes through
the TensorFlow model

00:23:49.730 --> 00:23:54.120
and applies the machine
learning, you'll see--

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:55.790
it's probably in
small text here--

00:23:55.790 --> 00:23:57.740
but you can see that it
has identified things

00:23:57.740 --> 00:24:01.610
like the vendor name, the
vendor tax registration

00:24:01.610 --> 00:24:07.350
number, the date of the
invoice, description, et cetera.

00:24:07.350 --> 00:24:11.180
On the right-hand side, we
have all the relevant fields

00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:14.243
for, in this case, an
Australian invoice.

00:24:14.243 --> 00:24:16.410
And again, given that we
have the content of the PWC

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:18.630
global network, we
know what fields

00:24:18.630 --> 00:24:20.340
are required for
what document type

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:25.080
in every location around the
world with a VAT or GST system.

00:24:25.080 --> 00:24:28.230
So you'll see here that
that TensorFlow model has

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:31.650
taken each field and identified
whether the value exists

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:33.160
within that invoice.

00:24:33.160 --> 00:24:35.500
So here, we do have
the phrase tax invoice,

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:36.790
which is a requirement.

00:24:36.790 --> 00:24:40.590
We've got the date, total
amount, GST amount included,

00:24:40.590 --> 00:24:43.830
the vendor's tax registration
number, et cetera.

00:24:43.830 --> 00:24:46.530
So as I said before, in
a matter of milliseconds,

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:51.470
we've taken an invoice, it's
been uploaded, and then OCRed,

00:24:51.470 --> 00:24:54.870
the ML model applied, and we
now have all the relevant fields

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:59.370
available for input into the
underlying system or validation

00:24:59.370 --> 00:25:00.930
of the underlying data import.

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:09.690
So all of those invoices
have now been uploaded,

00:25:09.690 --> 00:25:12.180
and we're then going to
visualize that through what we

00:25:12.180 --> 00:25:14.050
call the Swift Dashboard.

00:25:14.050 --> 00:25:17.940
So the Swift Dashboard is
where that stage two, ML,

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:18.840
has happened.

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:21.750
So this is where the expense
classification and validation

00:25:21.750 --> 00:25:22.560
is taking place.

00:25:25.610 --> 00:25:28.300
So you see here, we've
got some stats up top.

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:31.210
We've got how many
transactions have been uploaded

00:25:31.210 --> 00:25:35.110
into the solution, the total
value of transactions that's

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:37.990
been classified,
the total VAT spend

00:25:37.990 --> 00:25:42.100
on those transactions, and then
the resulting VAT claimable.

00:25:42.100 --> 00:25:45.100
So going back to Brady's
earlier point on live reporting,

00:25:45.100 --> 00:25:49.230
here we can take an entire data
set for a larger organization.

00:25:49.230 --> 00:25:52.390
And within a matter of minutes,
it's gone through the process,

00:25:52.390 --> 00:25:55.870
been validated, and is
producing a VAT claimable

00:25:55.870 --> 00:25:58.390
amount which can go on
a return or directly

00:25:58.390 --> 00:26:01.990
to the tax authorities.

00:26:01.990 --> 00:26:06.190
We've got some visualization
here over the amount, the VAT

00:26:06.190 --> 00:26:09.040
spend, and the categorizations.

00:26:09.040 --> 00:26:11.510
And as I mentioned before,
we're developing this further

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:14.805
with clients for a
client-user interface.

00:26:14.805 --> 00:26:16.180
But what I want
to do now is just

00:26:16.180 --> 00:26:18.850
take you through what
those five invoices were

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:21.430
and to show you how the
classification and validation

00:26:21.430 --> 00:26:24.850
process has worked.

00:26:24.850 --> 00:26:28.330
So I'm going to start with
one which is a valid invoice

00:26:28.330 --> 00:26:29.770
but is foreign.

00:26:29.770 --> 00:26:33.860
So a foreign
invoice is not going

00:26:33.860 --> 00:26:36.430
to be subject to
reclaim in Australia.

00:26:36.430 --> 00:26:40.490
If we have a look here, this
is an invoice from Singapore.

00:26:40.490 --> 00:26:41.860
It's a hotel invoice.

00:26:41.860 --> 00:26:43.450
This would be a
valid invoice if it

00:26:43.450 --> 00:26:45.730
was being incurred by
a Singaporean company

00:26:45.730 --> 00:26:46.720
in Singapore.

00:26:46.720 --> 00:26:49.360
But here, it's being
incurred by an Australian,

00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:53.540
so it's not claimable within
the Australian GST system.

00:26:53.540 --> 00:26:55.390
So if we click back
into Swift, we'll

00:26:55.390 --> 00:26:57.910
see here that it
was a valid invoice.

00:26:57.910 --> 00:27:01.810
However, it's been
classified as foreign.

00:27:01.810 --> 00:27:03.550
It's listed the total amount.

00:27:03.550 --> 00:27:05.460
It's listed the VAT spent.

00:27:05.460 --> 00:27:08.860
And here, it's made that
tax calculation decision

00:27:08.860 --> 00:27:10.990
that the tax claimable
is $0 on the basis

00:27:10.990 --> 00:27:12.970
that it's not local tax.

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:15.460
Interestingly enough, there
are a lot of cross-border tax

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:16.820
credit mechanisms.

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:18.970
So even though it's not
claimable within Australia,

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:21.640
we can then report for
potentially a Singapore reclaim

00:27:21.640 --> 00:27:23.440
where the client will
be able to claim that

00:27:23.440 --> 00:27:25.810
$73 back in a
separate tax return.

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:30.920
We move on to the next
example, and this is the one

00:27:30.920 --> 00:27:32.780
that we really try and avoid.

00:27:32.780 --> 00:27:35.067
This is where we've
got a local invoice,

00:27:35.067 --> 00:27:36.275
but it's not a valid invoice.

00:27:40.440 --> 00:27:43.940
If we have a look
at this example,

00:27:43.940 --> 00:27:46.780
This Is an Uber receipt.

00:27:46.780 --> 00:27:48.820
The value here is $83.

00:27:48.820 --> 00:27:52.870
In Australia, we have a rule
that anything above $82.50

00:27:52.870 --> 00:27:55.720
requires certain requirements
that aren't required under that

00:27:55.720 --> 00:27:56.650
threshold.

00:27:56.650 --> 00:27:58.900
So one of the requirements
is it must have its own tax

00:27:58.900 --> 00:28:00.760
invoice at the top.

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:04.690
You can see here that
the term invoice appears,

00:28:04.690 --> 00:28:07.750
but the term tax
invoice does not appear.

00:28:07.750 --> 00:28:11.110
As such, when we look at how
the solution has prefilled

00:28:11.110 --> 00:28:14.800
these fields, it does not apply
to value for the tax invoice

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:16.630
phrase appearing.

00:28:16.630 --> 00:28:19.520
On that basis, when we go
back into the solution,

00:28:19.520 --> 00:28:22.750
you can see that it's
been deemed to be invalid.

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:25.330
It's domestic.

00:28:25.330 --> 00:28:27.910
There is an amount of
tax on the invoice,

00:28:27.910 --> 00:28:31.108
but it's not claimable because
we have an invalid document.

00:28:31.108 --> 00:28:32.650
These are the types
of documents that

00:28:32.650 --> 00:28:34.525
are really useful from
a management reporting

00:28:34.525 --> 00:28:37.060
perspective to then inform
the control and process

00:28:37.060 --> 00:28:40.840
environment upstream about
what type of data and invoices

00:28:40.840 --> 00:28:44.650
is being provided by
either vendors or employees

00:28:44.650 --> 00:28:45.760
into the relevant systems.

00:28:45.760 --> 00:28:48.220
Because here,
we've paid tax out.

00:28:48.220 --> 00:28:50.110
We do have an implicit
right to claim it

00:28:50.110 --> 00:28:52.600
if we have a valid document,
but for whatever reason

00:28:52.600 --> 00:28:54.430
we don't hold that document.

00:28:54.430 --> 00:28:56.140
If we did, we'd
get the tax back.

00:28:56.140 --> 00:28:58.390
This is a massive gap
in our client base

00:28:58.390 --> 00:29:00.840
where they're paying
substantial amounts of tax out,

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:02.350
but the document is not valid.

00:29:05.700 --> 00:29:07.490
The third example
is a valid one where

00:29:07.490 --> 00:29:10.440
there was no GST on
the underlying invoice.

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:16.340
So if we have a look here, this
invoice formatting is perfect.

00:29:16.340 --> 00:29:18.380
it has all the required fields.

00:29:18.380 --> 00:29:21.260
But if we have a look, it
was international travel,

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:23.120
and therefore there was
no GST in the price.

00:29:25.772 --> 00:29:27.230
So the solution
has classified that

00:29:27.230 --> 00:29:30.830
is a valid invoice,
as a domestic invoice,

00:29:30.830 --> 00:29:33.800
but with no GST recovery
because there was no GST

00:29:33.800 --> 00:29:35.910
spend in the first place.

00:29:35.910 --> 00:29:38.625
One of the risks with
previous solutions is that,

00:29:38.625 --> 00:29:40.250
when you have a
category like airfare--

00:29:40.250 --> 00:29:43.550
which could potentially contain
tax or not depending on whether

00:29:43.550 --> 00:29:45.910
it's domestic versus
international travel--

00:29:45.910 --> 00:29:48.500
if you automatically assign
a tax credit calculation

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:50.540
to that airfare
category, that may

00:29:50.540 --> 00:29:53.750
have imputed a tax credit,
in this case, where

00:29:53.750 --> 00:29:55.970
there was no tax paid.

00:29:55.970 --> 00:29:59.150
So by going into the source
document and validating it,

00:29:59.150 --> 00:30:01.970
we take out any risk that
the underlying classification

00:30:01.970 --> 00:30:06.160
of that expense type account
may have been incorrect.

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:07.970
BRADY DEVER: And if
I could just add,

00:30:07.970 --> 00:30:11.060
so this is for us where
the real value occurs.

00:30:11.060 --> 00:30:13.190
So I mentioned
before, around systems

00:30:13.190 --> 00:30:15.920
having rule sets
to calculate GST,

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:19.460
we see time and time again the
system doing its job perfectly.

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:21.680
And what I mean by that
is, when the code is

00:30:21.680 --> 00:30:25.520
hit that says this is a
tax rate claimable expense,

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:27.150
everything works.

00:30:27.150 --> 00:30:29.060
The problem is, if
you're leaving it

00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:30.980
to an employee or an
accounts payable clerk

00:30:30.980 --> 00:30:33.350
to rely on that first decision
to get the categorization

00:30:33.350 --> 00:30:36.390
right, then that's
where the risk occurs.

00:30:36.390 --> 00:30:39.410
And so, what this solution does
is it scans the source document

00:30:39.410 --> 00:30:41.990
and works out from the source
document based on the ML model

00:30:41.990 --> 00:30:44.045
it sits behind what
kind of expense

00:30:44.045 --> 00:30:46.610
it is and is it classified
as a recoverable expense

00:30:46.610 --> 00:30:48.140
or a non-recoverable expense.

00:30:48.140 --> 00:30:51.560
So that gap where you're relying
on a non tax-trained person

00:30:51.560 --> 00:30:54.090
to make a tax call goes away.

00:30:54.090 --> 00:30:56.655
And that's where we see
the real value here.

00:30:56.655 --> 00:30:58.280
EDWARD KNUST: And as
business processes

00:30:58.280 --> 00:31:00.530
have become more and more
outsourced and more and more

00:31:00.530 --> 00:31:01.928
removed from the
business, that's

00:31:01.928 --> 00:31:04.220
how misclassification is
happening more and more often.

00:31:07.910 --> 00:31:11.970
So I'll run you through just the
last couple of examples here.

00:31:11.970 --> 00:31:16.180
I want to show you
a standard invoice--

00:31:16.180 --> 00:31:17.950
so one we looked at before.

00:31:17.950 --> 00:31:19.890
So as I mentioned
earlier on, all

00:31:19.890 --> 00:31:24.540
of the relevant requirements for
a valid tax invoice are here.

00:31:24.540 --> 00:31:28.650
So the solution has deemed
the invoice to be valid.

00:31:28.650 --> 00:31:31.050
It's a domestic invoice.

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:33.953
It's capable of full
VAT or GST recovery

00:31:33.953 --> 00:31:35.370
because here it's
a hotel invoice.

00:31:35.370 --> 00:31:37.123
It's a valid business spend.

00:31:37.123 --> 00:31:39.540
So you'll see here that the
VAT spend has been identified,

00:31:39.540 --> 00:31:41.250
and the VAT claimable
has been identified

00:31:41.250 --> 00:31:43.127
as exactly the same amount.

00:31:43.127 --> 00:31:45.210
So this is where we would
expect the vast majority

00:31:45.210 --> 00:31:47.490
of our clients'
invoices, this category,

00:31:47.490 --> 00:31:50.340
to fall into, where you
do have a valid document

00:31:50.340 --> 00:31:52.200
and you're
auto-calculating the tax.

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:53.700
But to Brady's
earlier point, you're

00:31:53.700 --> 00:31:57.000
getting the surety that,
on each and every occasion,

00:31:57.000 --> 00:31:59.820
the system is working correctly
rather than applying a default

00:31:59.820 --> 00:32:02.442
rule.

00:32:02.442 --> 00:32:03.900
And the last one
I want to show you

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:07.740
is where the real intelligent
part of that second ML model

00:32:07.740 --> 00:32:09.220
is coming in.

00:32:09.220 --> 00:32:14.980
So this is where we have
an entertainment expense.

00:32:14.980 --> 00:32:20.490
So here, we've got an invoice
or a receipt from a restaurant.

00:32:20.490 --> 00:32:23.480
The value shows that
it's not subsistence.

00:32:23.480 --> 00:32:26.000
I may or may not have
participated in that event.

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:28.720
It looked like it
was a good one.

00:32:28.720 --> 00:32:33.210
So here, that logic that's
applied within the ML

00:32:33.210 --> 00:32:36.090
model of looking at the
vendor type, of looking

00:32:36.090 --> 00:32:38.940
at the spend, the
location has been

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:42.000
able to determine that, rather
than being a general expense,

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:45.130
this is an expense of
an entertainment nature.

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:48.390
Now, entertainment typically
in most VAT jurisdictions

00:32:48.390 --> 00:32:51.180
has a restricted recovery
because the tax office doesn't

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:53.340
want you to be able
to go out and have

00:32:53.340 --> 00:32:57.600
a high-value lunch or dinner
and claim all the tax back.

00:32:57.600 --> 00:33:00.810
So here, we've identified that
it falls within that category.

00:33:00.810 --> 00:33:02.175
It is a valid document.

00:33:02.175 --> 00:33:04.050
So when it comes to the
VAT claimable amount,

00:33:04.050 --> 00:33:06.630
we've been able to apply
the relevant local rule

00:33:06.630 --> 00:33:08.200
for restriction.

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:11.510
So the VAT spend amount is
double what the VAT claimable

00:33:11.510 --> 00:33:15.090
bill amount is because we only
have a 50% right to recovery

00:33:15.090 --> 00:33:16.917
of entertainment expenses.

00:33:16.917 --> 00:33:18.750
This, again, is one of
those scenarios where

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:21.270
if you're an AP clerk
or an individual trying

00:33:21.270 --> 00:33:24.870
to do a classification in
an expense type which is not

00:33:24.870 --> 00:33:28.338
subject to full recovery,
that gets very, very tricky.

00:33:28.338 --> 00:33:29.880
So the ML model that
sits behind this

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:32.190
has been trained
for years by PWC

00:33:32.190 --> 00:33:33.810
to do expense type
classifications

00:33:33.810 --> 00:33:37.240
both for indirect taxes,
corporate, and other taxes.

00:33:37.240 --> 00:33:40.410
And so, we're able to apply it
here to do that classification,

00:33:40.410 --> 00:33:43.230
and we can use the same expense
classification methodology

00:33:43.230 --> 00:33:46.740
in any country that has
a differentiation on tax

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:50.740
credit of different
expense types incurred.

00:33:50.740 --> 00:33:53.290
BRADY DEVER: So what we've
done here in Australia

00:33:53.290 --> 00:33:55.060
is really just
categorize down to what

00:33:55.060 --> 00:33:58.120
we would call the lowest common
denominator from a GST recovery

00:33:58.120 --> 00:33:59.185
perspective.

00:33:59.185 --> 00:34:01.060
And we need to go deeper
and deeper depending

00:34:01.060 --> 00:34:02.150
on what the business is.

00:34:02.150 --> 00:34:04.240
So for example, financial
services entities

00:34:04.240 --> 00:34:08.710
in most VAT jurisdictions have
a really complex VAT recovery

00:34:08.710 --> 00:34:10.570
profile on purchases.

00:34:10.570 --> 00:34:13.480
And so, the Swift
classification logic--

00:34:13.480 --> 00:34:15.219
the PWC classification
logic-- that

00:34:15.219 --> 00:34:18.610
is deployed into
the Google ML model

00:34:18.610 --> 00:34:22.030
enables us to categorize,
just like what

00:34:22.030 --> 00:34:23.830
you saw on that
entertainment expense,

00:34:23.830 --> 00:34:27.190
to look at the individual
description of the line

00:34:27.190 --> 00:34:30.400
on the invoice, apply
business rules against that

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:32.367
to understand what
kind of expense it is

00:34:32.367 --> 00:34:34.659
and who's incurring it and
in what cost center and what

00:34:34.659 --> 00:34:38.440
department, and then apply
categorisation logic which

00:34:38.440 --> 00:34:42.520
then tags to a GST
or VAT result. OK?

00:34:42.520 --> 00:34:45.130
So that's where the tax
smarts really kick in

00:34:45.130 --> 00:34:47.710
and where the ML models
really, really serve us well,

00:34:47.710 --> 00:34:50.110
because we can access
hundreds of thousands

00:34:50.110 --> 00:34:52.900
or millions of
similar transactions

00:34:52.900 --> 00:34:55.150
posted by all of
our other clients--

00:34:55.150 --> 00:34:59.330
obviously, in a fully-sanitized
way to deal with privacy--

00:34:59.330 --> 00:35:01.030
to then effectively
come back out

00:35:01.030 --> 00:35:04.312
with a suggested classification
and categorization, which

00:35:04.312 --> 00:35:06.520
is really, really powerful
because, effectively, what

00:35:06.520 --> 00:35:10.420
we're now doing is moving away
from rules-based tax decisions

00:35:10.420 --> 00:35:13.990
to model-based tax decisions,
which is really exciting.

00:35:18.570 --> 00:35:20.580
EDWARD KNUST: So that's
the end of the demo.

00:35:20.580 --> 00:35:22.100
We thought we
would then take you

00:35:22.100 --> 00:35:25.670
through where we're going
to take the solution

00:35:25.670 --> 00:35:26.920
over the next couple of years.

00:35:31.600 --> 00:35:34.560
So the product development
roadmap for the rest of 2019,

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:36.960
as we discussed earlier, we
finished the proof of concept

00:35:36.960 --> 00:35:38.350
earlier this year.

00:35:38.350 --> 00:35:41.820
We've now gone live with this
as a live client solution

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:44.220
within Australia.

00:35:44.220 --> 00:35:46.860
We're very quickly
localizing the solution

00:35:46.860 --> 00:35:49.830
in additional jurisdictions.

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:52.342
This will be our
global solution,

00:35:52.342 --> 00:35:53.550
so we're moving very quickly.

00:35:53.550 --> 00:35:56.850
I'll talk about those
locations in the next slide.

00:35:56.850 --> 00:36:00.420
And next, as I mentioned
earlier, the API configuration

00:36:00.420 --> 00:36:04.140
to expense management systems
we see as key in the short term

00:36:04.140 --> 00:36:06.360
because, typically,
our clients really

00:36:06.360 --> 00:36:08.130
struggle with the
extraction of data--

00:36:08.130 --> 00:36:11.010
so being able to have APIs into
the main expense management

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:16.970
systems, including the SAPs,
SAP Concur, Oracle, et cetera.

00:36:16.970 --> 00:36:18.500
All of those main
expense systems

00:36:18.500 --> 00:36:22.800
will be looking to
configure into their APIs.

00:36:22.800 --> 00:36:27.600
And UI enhancement, as pretty
as this looks or did look,

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:29.130
we're very conscious
that clients

00:36:29.130 --> 00:36:32.265
do want value-added
analytics and reporting.

00:36:32.265 --> 00:36:34.890
As Brady was mentioning earlier,
if you think about the expense

00:36:34.890 --> 00:36:37.218
classification analysis
that we've been able to do,

00:36:37.218 --> 00:36:38.760
some of our clients
have talked to us

00:36:38.760 --> 00:36:41.100
about using that for
broader management reporting

00:36:41.100 --> 00:36:43.890
because a lot of them don't
have visibility themselves

00:36:43.890 --> 00:36:46.020
over the different
classifications of expense

00:36:46.020 --> 00:36:49.320
spend or potentially
misclassifications.

00:36:49.320 --> 00:36:54.100
So broader use cases we're
looking into as well.

00:36:54.100 --> 00:36:57.480
So I'll move onto our
localization strategy.

00:36:57.480 --> 00:36:58.480
BRADY DEVER: Sorry.

00:36:58.480 --> 00:37:00.390
I know this back and
forth probably isn't--

00:37:00.390 --> 00:37:03.060
we should have really prepared
for the back and forth.

00:37:03.060 --> 00:37:03.990
I apologize.

00:37:03.990 --> 00:37:07.390
The one thing I just
going to add was the API

00:37:07.390 --> 00:37:09.510
and configuration
into the ERPs--

00:37:09.510 --> 00:37:11.190
and I kind of touched
on it earlier--

00:37:11.190 --> 00:37:13.560
really, really critical
for us because this

00:37:13.560 --> 00:37:16.270
is one part of a broader
compliance process stream

00:37:16.270 --> 00:37:17.400
for tax.

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:20.530
So we've got calculation
in the system.

00:37:20.530 --> 00:37:24.410
And then, that data then
moves into reporting suites.

00:37:24.410 --> 00:37:29.310
And SAP and Oracle in particular
are developing their capability

00:37:29.310 --> 00:37:32.550
around tax reporting and
filing out of the ERP

00:37:32.550 --> 00:37:34.580
as opposed to extraction
and then filing

00:37:34.580 --> 00:37:37.990
via a third-party solution.

00:37:37.990 --> 00:37:39.990
And so, what this is
really, really exciting

00:37:39.990 --> 00:37:42.780
about is this enables
end-to-end automation.

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:45.210
And PWC actually
is working with SAP

00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:50.280
as well to build tax reporting
solutions inside of the ERP

00:37:50.280 --> 00:37:53.070
to do the filing to
the regulator, some

00:37:53.070 --> 00:37:57.610
of the post-filing analytics
and reconciliations and trending

00:37:57.610 --> 00:37:58.980
and variance testing.

00:37:58.980 --> 00:38:04.380
And so, by having backend
automation inside of the ERP

00:38:04.380 --> 00:38:07.710
to do tax compliance
and reporting combined

00:38:07.710 --> 00:38:10.140
with tax calculation at
the frontend, all inside

00:38:10.140 --> 00:38:12.730
of the system, is a really,
really exciting prospect

00:38:12.730 --> 00:38:13.230
for us.

00:38:13.230 --> 00:38:19.140
And it's enabled by GCP
and Google AI and ML

00:38:19.140 --> 00:38:23.840
through the collaboration
that PWC, SAP, and Google

00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:25.390
are partaking in at the moment.

00:38:25.390 --> 00:38:27.380
So really, really
exciting times.

00:38:27.380 --> 00:38:27.880
Sorry.

00:38:27.880 --> 00:38:29.263
EDWARD KNUST: That's all right.

00:38:32.452 --> 00:38:33.660
So our localization strategy.

00:38:33.660 --> 00:38:34.660
We've said in Australia.

00:38:34.660 --> 00:38:38.850
We're sort of heading
northwest through the world.

00:38:38.850 --> 00:38:42.760
We're localizing for
three different reasons.

00:38:42.760 --> 00:38:45.337
So the strategy is driven by
the complexity of tax rules.

00:38:45.337 --> 00:38:46.920
So we certainly
believe that we should

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:48.755
tackle complex
jurisdictions early

00:38:48.755 --> 00:38:50.880
because, if you can do it
in complex jurisdictions,

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:52.440
you can do it in anywhere.

00:38:52.440 --> 00:38:54.990
And the EU and
Canada are examples

00:38:54.990 --> 00:38:57.370
of particularly
complex jurisdictions.

00:38:57.370 --> 00:39:01.090
So those localizations
are already underway.

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:02.220
The value of tax at stake.

00:39:02.220 --> 00:39:04.740
Clearly, our clients
want us to localize

00:39:04.740 --> 00:39:08.160
where they have the biggest
tax risk or biggest tax

00:39:08.160 --> 00:39:11.140
amount sitting on the
table not being collected.

00:39:11.140 --> 00:39:15.010
So an example of that in the
Asia-Pac region is Singapore.

00:39:15.010 --> 00:39:19.350
So Singapore is the hub for
firms in the region which

00:39:19.350 --> 00:39:21.030
have a VAT and GST.

00:39:21.030 --> 00:39:22.110
Hong Kong does not.

00:39:22.110 --> 00:39:25.020
So Singaporean-based clients are
very keen to get this solution,

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:27.300
so we're localizing there.

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:30.990
And India is an example of
where you have very complex

00:39:30.990 --> 00:39:33.420
local country validation rules.

00:39:33.420 --> 00:39:37.500
So the process for claiming
tax credits based on invoices

00:39:37.500 --> 00:39:39.280
in India is very complex.

00:39:39.280 --> 00:39:41.620
So we're localizing
there as well.

00:39:41.620 --> 00:39:43.770
So by localizing in those
different countries which

00:39:43.770 --> 00:39:46.710
have those different
complexities and requirements,

00:39:46.710 --> 00:39:48.600
we're confident
that we will then

00:39:48.600 --> 00:39:52.860
have a product which is
truly globally capable based

00:39:52.860 --> 00:39:55.140
on those first five countries.

00:39:55.140 --> 00:39:57.210
And certainly, we're
looking to replicate

00:39:57.210 --> 00:40:00.300
what we've already done
from global VAT reporting

00:40:00.300 --> 00:40:01.403
automation--

00:40:01.403 --> 00:40:03.570
for which we've had a
solution in place for a number

00:40:03.570 --> 00:40:04.680
of years--

00:40:04.680 --> 00:40:09.480
into this upstream source system
classification, validation,

00:40:09.480 --> 00:40:10.743
and calculation.

00:40:14.610 --> 00:40:17.970
So we'll finish just
on future use cases,

00:40:17.970 --> 00:40:20.190
and I'd be keen to
hear anyone's ideas

00:40:20.190 --> 00:40:22.080
because we're always
open to new ideas.

00:40:22.080 --> 00:40:25.600
Our clients are always
challenging us with new ideas.

00:40:25.600 --> 00:40:29.400
So the obvious one to us is
any other tax applications

00:40:29.400 --> 00:40:33.090
where the underlying
document is relevant for tax

00:40:33.090 --> 00:40:33.780
classification.

00:40:33.780 --> 00:40:37.120
So here, we're talking about
withholding tax, stamp taxes,

00:40:37.120 --> 00:40:41.370
sales taxes, you're having
to make an OPEX versus CAPEX

00:40:41.370 --> 00:40:43.410
decision which has a tax impact.

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:47.730
Any of these taxes which rely
on a document for calculation

00:40:47.730 --> 00:40:49.740
and validation are
prime candidates,

00:40:49.740 --> 00:40:53.160
and our clients are
asking us to build those.

00:40:53.160 --> 00:40:56.730
More broadly, in finance process
automation, many of our clients

00:40:56.730 --> 00:40:59.060
are going through those
Cloud ERP upgrades--

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:01.140
[INAUDIBLE]
upgrades, et cetera--

00:41:01.140 --> 00:41:03.420
and this is the
perfect time for them

00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:05.850
to do that last
leg of automation,

00:41:05.850 --> 00:41:08.250
which isn't necessarily
available within those Cloud

00:41:08.250 --> 00:41:08.985
ERP suites.

00:41:11.880 --> 00:41:15.390
And thirdly, broader
business process automation.

00:41:15.390 --> 00:41:16.570
I mean, the sky's the limit.

00:41:16.570 --> 00:41:20.220
Anything within a business
process, which involves someone

00:41:20.220 --> 00:41:24.000
having to use a document to make
a decision or a classification

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:27.540
or a validation Zipline
can be applied to.

00:41:27.540 --> 00:41:30.000
So we're keen to
get the core use

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:33.390
case bedded down and localized
across those five countries,

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:36.990
but we really see the ability
to have multiple streams

00:41:36.990 --> 00:41:39.690
and multiple different
use cases of Zipline

00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:40.938
in the very near future.

00:41:40.938 --> 00:41:43.230
BRADY DEVER: And I think it's
useful to call out in two

00:41:43.230 --> 00:41:46.380
and three, in particular-- well,
across all of those three use

00:41:46.380 --> 00:41:47.010
cases--

00:41:47.010 --> 00:41:49.020
these are real
examples of clients

00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:51.150
in the very, very
early embryonic stages

00:41:51.150 --> 00:41:54.470
of this project that
are starting to ask us,

00:41:54.470 --> 00:41:56.230
could it do this,
could it do that?

00:41:56.230 --> 00:41:58.710
And so, we've had clients ask
us about withholding taxes.

00:41:58.710 --> 00:42:00.293
We've got one client,
we were actually

00:42:00.293 --> 00:42:03.150
doing an augmentation of
this to do accounts payable

00:42:03.150 --> 00:42:06.840
processing-- so to actually
process the expense

00:42:06.840 --> 00:42:09.810
and replace the existing process
altogether, not just do the tax

00:42:09.810 --> 00:42:10.500
calc.

00:42:10.500 --> 00:42:12.450
Really exciting stuff,
particularly when

00:42:12.450 --> 00:42:14.670
you see the cost of
invoice processing, which

00:42:14.670 --> 00:42:17.280
I think we had on one of our
earliest slides of around $5 US

00:42:17.280 --> 00:42:17.790
an invoice.

00:42:19.873 --> 00:42:21.540
The other thing that
we've started to do

00:42:21.540 --> 00:42:25.350
is work together with some of
our own forensics and analytics

00:42:25.350 --> 00:42:28.890
teams outside of
tax, and we're doing

00:42:28.890 --> 00:42:31.020
things in terms of
complementing some existing

00:42:31.020 --> 00:42:33.840
processes around loan
document analysis

00:42:33.840 --> 00:42:38.420
for banks, expense fraud
analysis just given that

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:43.308
the power of the data that
we've got in this model.

00:42:43.308 --> 00:42:43.850
And so, yeah.

00:42:43.850 --> 00:42:50.130
Really, we see this is as a
little, humble indirect tax

00:42:50.130 --> 00:42:53.040
solution that has much,
much greater application

00:42:53.040 --> 00:42:57.690
outside of indirect taxes and
huge potential for our clients.

00:42:57.690 --> 00:43:00.740
[MUSIC PLAYING]

